  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1259 AM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nLow clouds have covered much of southwest Kansas this morning in\nthe wake of a cold front. Elevated t-storms formed north of \nDodge City and Garden City along a mid level baroclinic zone as \na shortwave trough passes across the central plains. This\nprecipitation will be out southwest Kansas by 3-4 am. Cooler \nweather is forecast for today, with low clouds gradually burning\noff with daytime heating. Expect highs near 80 along I-70, with\nlower to mid 90s along the KS/OK state line.\n\nBy tonight the aforementioned cold front will lift back\nnorthward as a warm front into southwest Kansas. Moistening at\nlow and mid levels may result in elevated instability and \nthunderstorm development. Rain amounts are very uncertain, with\nsome model guidance showing more coverage than others. The\ncurrent forecast has 20-40% storm chances and this seems\nreasonable. \n\nWest northwesterly mid level flow is forecast to persist on\nThursday as another shortwave trough traverses the northern\nplains. Of course, given some mid level level wind component\nacross the Rockies, surface troughing is expected to develop on\nthe high plains, with a nearly stationary front persisting over\nsouthwest Kansas. An isolated late afternoon t-storm or two \ncan't be ruled out in this pattern; and any storms could \ndevelop into a larger cluster during the evening and overnight.\nAny early evening storms could be severe given the moderate \nsurface based CAPE immediately on the cool side of the front \nand strongly veering wind profile. However, there is \nconsiderable uncertainty among the various model guidance suites\nabout how widespread overnight rains may be. The Grand Ensemble\nindicates very low probabilities (10-30%) of .25\" or more of \nrain for Thursday night and Friday morning. But of course,\namounts would be much higher in any localized t-storms.\n\nA front will linger across southwest Kansas even into Friday. \nHowever, mid level (700mb) temperatures are expected to warm to\naround 15C; so it will take full heating just to get one or two\nisolated late day t-storms in the absence of upper level \nsupport. But given the proximity of the front, small storm \nchances (5-15% at any given location) are still warranted.\n\nThe upper level ridge is forecast to amplify across the high\nplains by Saturday, with the low level frontal zone retreating \nwell north of I-70. As is typical in summer on the high plains,\nthe lower levels will dry out as the boundary layer deepens \nand temperatures heat back up, leading to deep inverted-v type \nsoundings and negligible instability. Thus, Saturday will be a \ndry and hot day, with readings 100+. However, given the dry air,\nheat indices will be at or less than the actual temperatures. \nHot and dry conditions are forecast through Monday, with \nafternoon highs from 100-105 and heat indices from 98 to 105.\n\nThe various ensemble means differ somewhat with the amplitude \nand location of the upper level ridge by mid to late next week,\nalong with the frontal location. A stronger ridge would lead to\ncontinued very hot and dry weather while a weaker ridge would \nbring moisture southward into Kansas, along with small t-storm \nchances and slightly cooler temperatures.",
